# bc_admits.ledger — PROGRAMS rustc REJECTS AND logosc COMPILES, named one per
# row. THIS FILE IS THE ONLY PLACE THE ADMITTED SET IS NAMED.
#
# Source: 983 rustc compile-fail tests from tests/ui/{borrowck,nll,moves,
# lifetimes,regions,dropck,drop}, ported at upstream
# da5114692c9ebe46b869488c5f34f92eb10b98c1 (src/version 1.100.0), batch B167.
# The ports that logosc refuses FOR THE UPSTREAM REASON are ordinary fail
# fixtures under tests/imported/fail/ and are not listed here. These are the
# rest: each is a borrow-check hole, and the `root` column is the root cause the
# import blocks isolated with a control that refuses.
#
# Format:  <fixture> <root-id> <path relative to the repo root, no .logos>
#
# `root-id` is BLOCK-QUALIFIED (`bck.` / `nllmoves.` / `lifereg.`) because the
# class letters are per-block names, not global identities — the same letter
# means different things in two blocks, and a bare `B` would silently merge
# them. Roots, per the block reports:
#   A  a PARAMETER is not in the tracked set          D  a TEMPORARY has no owner to key a loan on
#   B  bookkeeping is ROOT-keyed and does not follow  E  a DROP-ORDER interaction is not modelled
#      a projection (field / index / deref hop)       L* lifetime-specific roots (lifereg block)
#   C  the CLOSURE BOUNDARY deposits nothing          N*/R*/NEW* roots proposed by their slice
#
# Held in BOTH directions by `logos_00_bc_admits_ledger`
# (tests/logos/bc_admits_ledger_gate.sh): a listed program that STOPS being
# admitted is RED — a fixed defect must DELETE its row and land the program as
# a fail fixture. The `# TOTAL` line below is held against the row count, so
# neither adding nor removing a row can be silent.
#
# ⚠ A ROW IS NOT AN EXCUSE. It is a defect with a name, and the intended way for
# this file to change is to SHRINK: a class fix reds this gate BECAUSE IT
# IMPROVED, and the count is then edited down in the same change.
# 2026-08-26 — 455 -> 452. THE LEDGER SHRANK BECAUSE THE CHECKER IMPROVED, which
# is the only direction this file is meant to move. The D1 by-value-hop gate in
# take_ref_borrows asked `loan_carrying_type` — the NAMED-CARRIER closure, which
# has no Ref/MutRef/Slice arm — while every other "should I look for hop roots"
# site in that file asks `type_may_carry_borrow`. A value typed plain `&T` never
# reached bc_hop_roots. Delegating the outer gate to the wide predicate closed
# THREE rows that no round set out to close, each for its upstream reason:
#   issue-48803                       E0506  "cannot assign to 'x' because it is borrowed"
#   ...--f-nested-holder              E0506  "cannot assign to 'x' while it is mutably borrowed"
#   polonius-smoke-test               E0505  "cannot move 's' while it is borrowed"
# All three moved to tests/imported/fail/nll/ with those diagnostics pinned; the
# rows are deleted here, not commented out.
# 2026-08-26 — 452 -> 451. ONE row, not the ten a survey of class C predicted:
# take_ref_borrows' Call and MethodCall arms filtered their arguments by a
# narrow TYPE test while the StructLit/TupleLit/ArrLit arms recurse
# unconditionally, so a `Kind::Closure` argument passed neither disjunct and the
# capture-deposit code was never reached for a closure handed to a call.
# Delegating both arms to `retains_borrowing_operand` closed exactly
#   borrowck-closures-two-mut       E0499  cannot borrow 'x' as mutable: already mutably borrowed
# and NONE of the other nine rows the survey grouped as C-beta. That is the
# survey's own stated floor being right and its upper bound being wrong: those
# rows are blocked by MORE THAN ONE mechanism, and a row retires only when all
# of its blockers close. Recorded as measured, not as predicted.
# 2026-08-27 — 451 -> 447. FOUR rows, and the number was PREDICTED AS FOUR
# before the edit — against all 451 rows, not against the 82 that share the
# class-B label. take_ref_borrows deposited every borrow through one of two
# recorders chosen by hand at sixteen call sites, with FOUR different spellings
# of one exemption beside them: take_borrow's `skip_mut_binding_check`, the
# reborrow arm's `fake_param`, `MutBindBypass`, and take_field_borrow's
# `root_is_{mut,shared}_ref`. All four key on the ROOT or on the BINDING. The
# question is about neither: a `&mut` is legal iff the REFERENCE the place is
# reached THROUGH is a `&mut`. BorrowPlace now records that reference's type
# and the single record site asks it once for both tails:
#   borrowck-access-permissions--d-mut-borrow-deref-shared-ref             E0596
#   borrowck-borrow-mut-base-ptr-in-aliasable-loc--c-mut-reborrow-from-shared E0596
#   borrow-raw-address-of-deref-mutability                                 E0596
#   borrowck-closures-mut-of-imm--ctl                                      E0596
# All four moved to tests/imported/fail/borrowck/ with the diagnostic pinned.
# ⚠ AND THE SHARED SYMPTOM IS AGAIN NOT THE SHARED DEFECT. Class B is 82 rows
# glossed "bookkeeping is ROOT-keyed and does not follow a projection"; this
# closed FOUR of them. 76 have no record-side mechanism at all — they are
# pattern-binding sites that deposit nothing, places whose base is not a
# variable, region/dropck rows, and read-side rows. The remaining two with this
# spelling (borrowck-access-permissions--c-mut-borrow-deref-box,
# borrow-immutable-deref-box) are NOT closed and were not attempted: a `Box`
# deref crosses no reference, so closing them means deleting
# `skip_mut_binding_check`, which take_borrow's own comment says the stdlib's
# `arc.deref_mut()` on a non-mut Arc binding relies on. That is its own round,
# with the stdlib build as the oracle.
# 2026-08-27 — 447 -> 438. NINE rows; the CEILING PROBE said 36 and the
# prediction before the edit said 9. The four pattern propagators (sources /
# prov / loans / reborrows) all COPY facts the scrutinee already carries onto
# a binding; not one RAISES a loan. So `match x { Opt::Some(ref r) => { x =
# …; *r } }` compiled while the by-value twin `let r = &x;` refuses one line
# over. A `ref` / `ref mut` binding now records a borrow of the sub-place
# `each_pat_binding_place` already computes, shared or mutable as the keyword
# says, held by the binding and released when the arm's scope pops.
#   borrowck-anon-fields-variant                        E0499/E0503
#   borrowck-lend-flow-match                            E0506
#   borrowck-pat-reassign-binding                       E0506
#   borrowck-ref-mut-of-imm--d-control-ref-mut-on-local  E0596
#   borrowck-slice-pattern-element-loan-slice           E0502
#   borrowck-vec-pattern-nesting                        E0506
#   issue-27282-move-ref-mut-into-guard                 E0507
#   issue-27282-reborrow-ref-mut-in-guard               E0596
#   regions-pattern-typing-issue-19997                  E0506
# All nine moved to tests/imported/fail/ with the diagnostic pinned.
# ⚠ A TENTH CLOSED AND WAS PUT BACK. `issue-51117` refuses only when the rule
# also covers RFC-2005 DEFAULT-mode bindings, and that half over-refuses: the
# place an ergonomic binding names sits under the scrutinee's IMPLICIT DEREF,
# so keying its loan on the reference LOCAL made `match cur { List::Cons(v,
# rest) => { cur = &**rest; } }` refuse — MEASURED as two reds in
# tests/spec/pass (type_3, type_8), a legal list walk. The rule was narrowed to
# the WRITTEN keyword, issue-51117 went back on this shelf with its row, and
# pass/bc_patloan_ergonomic_ref_local_reassign_admit pins the shape that bought
# it. The ergonomic half needs the loan keyed on the POINTEE — a read-side
# change and its own round.
# ⚠ THE GATE IS THE BINDING MODE, NOT THE BINDING'S TYPE, and that distinction
# is a new CARRIED FACT (lir_schema::pat_keys::BINDING_REF_MODES). `E::V(p)`
# over `enum E { V(&i64) }` and `Opt::Some(ref p)` over `Opt::Some(i64)` give
# the binding the identical type `&i64`; only the first is a copy. A rule keyed
# on the type refuses the copy, and pass/bc_patloan_byvalue_ref_payload_admit
# is the fixture that says so.
# ⚠ 27 of the probe's 36 did NOT close, and that gap is the probe being
# deliberately wrong: it recorded a MUTABLE loan for EVERY binding, by-value
# ones included. Those 27 are move-out-of-a-reference and closure-escape rows —
# a different mechanism, not a weaker version of this one. Class B goes 82 -> 73.
# 2026-08-27 — 438 -> 430. DROPCK LIVENESS ENTERS THE LOAN CHANNEL. The ceiling
# probe `nll_lu_strict` said 12 and the one-character `'<=' -> '<'` it stood for
# is REFUTED: re-measured on the pass corpus it breaks 14 legal programs
# (tests/spec/pass/{borrow_1,borrow_2,coerce_4,coerce_5,expr_2,expr_6,expr_12,
# mono_6} among them) to buy at most 12 rows — a net loss, and the cursor's `<=`
# is CORRECT (a use is charged its enclosing statement's point and the release
# sweep runs after that statement was visited).
# The real mechanism under those 12: a binding whose type has a `Drop` impl is
# used ONE MORE TIME after its textual last use — at its drop. `release_dead_
# borrows` reads only `last_use_*`, which knows nothing about drops, so `let w =
# Wrap { p: &mut x }; x = 1;` retired the loan at the `let`. MEASURED with
# LOGOS_DUMP_BC_RELEASE: `holder=wrap lu=0`. `drop_can_observe_borrow` now holds
# such a loan open; it dies at `pop_scope`, which IS the drop point, so no new
# lifetime concept is minted.
# ⚠ THE REACH TEST IS DIRECT DECLARED FIELDS ONLY, AND THAT IS THE `may_dangle`
# STAND-IN. `Vec<B>`/`Box<&T>` reach their elements too but their drops are the
# `#[may_dangle]` kind; `bc_loan_carrying_type`'s TYPE-ARG recursion is exactly
# what would capture them and is deliberately not used. Residual, stated: a
# Drop-bearing CONTAINER of borrows stays admitted.
# EXEMPTIONS, MEASURED as pairs against the crude probes rather than argued:
# a non-Drop holder (pass/bc_dropck_loan_nodrop_admit) and a Drop impl the
# borrow does not reach — a RAW-POINTER field (pass/bc_dropck_loan_raw_field_
# admit, the 12-line minimisation of tests/spec/pass/mono_6, which BOTH crude
# probes reddened). Predicted 5, closed 8: the three move-out rows
# (issue-54556-wrap-it-up, the two fragment-drop rows) followed their holder.
# Class B (roots ending `.B`, counted by direct listing) 69 -> 67. ⚠ the previous
# round's note said 73; a direct listing of HEAD said 69, so 69 is what is
# recorded here — the number, re-measured, not carried forward.
# 2026-08-27 — 430 -> 426. THE WRITE PATH INHERITS THE QUESTION THE BORROW PATH
# ALREADY ASKS. A `&mut` taken through a SHARED reference is E0596 and a WRITE
# through one is E0594: the same question about the same BorrowPlace, computed
# by the same walker (`cross()` records which reference was crossed).
# record_borrow asks it at THE ONE RECORD SITE, before the whole/field dispatch;
# `check_place_mut_use` — the only checker of a `*place = v` write — never
# inherited it. The spread was directly observable on ONE program in two
# spellings, unarmed: `&mut *s.p` refused, `*s.p = 7` compiled. No predicate is
# minted here, one is delegated.
# THE RAW-POINTER EXEMPTION IS WHAT THE RULE RESTS ON, and it was measured at
# the guard boundary by FIRE COUNT, not argued: `struct S { p: *mut i64 }` with
# `unsafe { *s.p = 7 }` has ZERO arrivals at the site (the guard one line above
# returns first), while the byte-identical program with `p: &mut i64` has one
# and refuses. Abuse direction: entering the hatch costs an `unsafe` block,
# enforced independently. All four stdlib layers compile clean against a clean
# unarmed control; the core pass corpus showed 0 over-refusals.
# ⚠ ASKED BEFORE THE ROOT-EMPTY RETURN, deliberately: "behind a shared `&`" is a
# property of the PATH, not of the loan table. `**y = 2` over a Box loses its
# root at the user-Deref call while the crossing is already recorded — 2 of
# these 4 rows, which a root-empty guard "for symmetry" would drop silently.
# CEILING 4, PREDICTED 4, CLOSED 4. Equality is the boundary case and it is not
# luck: the probe was placed UNDER this fix's only exemption, so the correct
# version differs from it in the diagnostic text and nothing else.
# ⚠ NOT A CLOSED CLASS. `cross()` is last-assignment-wins, so `s: &mut S,
# s.r: &i64, *s.r = 5` is still admitted — priced at 0 rows by `sharedsticky`.
# Class B 67 -> 63.
# 2026-08-27 — 426 -> 423. ONE WRONG COMMENT IN pop_scope's B87 DROPCK BLOCK.
# It read "skip if the binding itself is being declared/erased here — its own
# death coincides with the source, no issue". FALSE: drop order inside ONE frame
# is REVERSE DECLARATION ORDER, so a Drop-having binding declared BEFORE its
# source is dropped AFTER it and its `drop` reads a local that is already dead.
# The rule therefore only ever saw the CROSS-FRAME case and was blind to the
# entire same-frame population. `let h: H; let x = 7i64; h = H{r:&x};` — the
# three imported dropck fixtures are literally that program and their own
# headers say so. Same shape as the previous round's landing: what decides is a
# POSITION, not membership in a set. `dying_binding` is now expressed in terms
# of `declared_pos` so the boolean and the position cannot drift.
# Nothing new is tracked: `dropck_borrow_sources_`, `struct_is_dropck_relevant`,
# `ScopeFrame::declared` and the F5 slot predicate all already shipped.
# ⚠ THE CEILING PROBE `droporder` WAS TWO RULES WELDED INTO ONE and its 9 means
# nothing until split. Split, the halves are DISJOINT and partition it exactly:
# same-frame reverse order (6 crude) and report-at-the-deposit (3). The crude
# reverse half is UNLANDABLE — un-gated it refuses `let r: &i64; let x = 7i64;
# r = &x;`, deferred initialisation of an ordinary reference, and a struct with
# a reference field but no `Drop` impl. Both MEASURED as one-variable pairs.
# Gating on `dropck_borrow_sources_` (which is written only under
# `struct_is_dropck_relevant`) buys 3 and breaks neither; the pair
# pass/bc_dropck_reverse_order_nodrop_admit against
# fail/bc_dropck_reverse_order_fail is what ratchets that.
# CEILING 9 (whole probe, re-priced), PREDICTED 3, CLOSED 3.
# THE OTHER 6 ARE NOT THIS MECHANISM, and the gated fix leaving them admitted is
# the evidence: slice-index-bounds-check-invalidation--t35 is E0510 with no Drop
# anywhere (the crude probe closed it by accident, on declaration order alone);
# let-else-more-permissive and eager-by-ref-binding-for-guards are dropck via a
# FIELD WRITE `long1.r = &short1`, which neither recording site fires on — a
# MISSING PRODUCER, one clean 2-row mechanism for #142; issue-54556-stephaneyfx
# has no declared lifetime parameter so `struct_is_dropck_relevant` is false,
# blocked upstream by the elided-lifetime gate; ex3-both-anon-regions-2 is
# out-param provenance; regions-escape-unboxed-closure is a stored borrow never
# read after, which NLL admits by design (DIVERGENCES B6).
# ⚠ RESIDUAL, PRE-EXISTING AND NOW REACHABLE FROM SAME-FRAME CODE: the re-assign
# arm overwrites `dropck_borrow_sources_` only `if (!sources.empty())`, so
# re-binding a dropck struct to a value that borrows nothing leaves the old
# sources standing. Not widened here, not exercised by any of the 426 rows.
# Class B unchanged at 63 — all three rows are lifereg.E.
# 2026-08-27 — 423 -> 412. ELEVEN rows; PREDICTED 12 and one of the twelve was
# PUT BACK, on the full-conformance run, because closing it was an over-refusal.
# E0507 was asked in sema from FIVE HAND-LISTED POSITIONS (let RHS, `return`,
# tail-expr return, arg coercion, array destructure) through
# `is_unowned_move_source`, whose `Deref` arm additionally requires the operand
# to be SPELLED `VarRef`. Neither the position list nor the spelling is the
# property. `consuming` — which borrow_check's `visit` already computes once and
# propagates — IS the position-general answer, so the rule now lives at the
# DEREF, in one place, and a position it has never seen cannot escape it:
#   borrowck-move-out-of-static-item                       static item 'BAR'
#   issue-17718-static-move                                static item 'FOO'
#   issue-47215-ice-from-drop-elab--move-out-of-static     static item 'X'
#   issue-47215-ice-from-drop-elab--b-move-out-of-static-twice  static item 'X'
#   issue-47215-ice-from-drop-elab--r-runtime              static item 'X'
#   move-error-snippets--r31                               static item 'D'
#   move-error-snippets--t31                               static item 'D'
#   move-from-union-field-issue-66500  `*u.a`      operand FieldRead, not VarRef
#   move-errors--c                     `**r`       operand Deref,     not VarRef
#   issue-20801                        `*mut_ref(&mut t)`  operand Call
#   cannot-move-block-spans            `let x = { *r };`   position: block tail
# All eleven moved to tests/imported/fail/ with the diagnostic pinned.
# ⚠ THE SEVEN STATIC ROWS ARE ONE LINE, AND IT IS THE EXEMPTION, NOT THE RULE.
# A `static` READ lowers to `Deref(VarRef("__static_addr:<sym>", *T))`, so its
# operand is a RAW POINTER by spelling. `deref_move_exempt` answers `false` for
# that name before it ever looks at the type — the programmer wrote no pointer,
# and rustc's answer is E0507 "cannot move out of static item".
# ⚠ THE 12th ROW WAS CLOSED, MEASURED, AND HANDED BACK. `move-errors--d` is
# `let A { s: v } = *r;` and the crude probe, the prediction and the first build
# all counted it. The full-conformance run (L4 with `imp`) then reddened
# tests/imported/pass/structs/newtype-struct-with-dtor — `let Fd(s) = *self;`
# over `pub struct Fd(u32)` with a `Drop` impl, a rustc UI PASS test, because
# `u32` is Copy and the pattern therefore MOVES NOTHING. Its shape is
# `move-errors--d`'s shape exactly, and our port of move-errors--d gave the
# field type `i64` where upstream has `String`. A destructuring `let` lowers to
# `let __dst_N: A = *r;` plus field reads, so the position no longer knows which
# it is: the exemption is on the POSITION and the row goes back on the shelf.
# Closing it means carrying the pattern's move-ness onto the temp's `let` — a
# sema change and its own round. THIS IS WHY THE GATE INCLUDES `imp` FOR A
# BORROW-CHECK ROUND: the 478-test cost corpus and plain L4 were both green.
# ⚠ THE 13th ROW OF THE PROBE'S CEILING IS DELIBERATELY LEFT STANDING.
# `borrowck-move-from-unsafe-ptr` is `let y = *x;` over a `*const Own`, and
# refusing it means refusing the raw-pointer deref-move that logos.mem's ptr /
# Vec / Cell primitives are built on (`let old = *p; *p = new;`).
# `is_unowned_move_source` takes the same exemption for the same stated reason.
# It is a DIVERGENCE with a name on this shelf, not an oversight: CEILING 13,
# PREDICTED 12, CLOSED 11, and both rows left standing are rows a refusal could
# only have been bought with an over-refusal.
# ⚠ A FOURTH EXEMPTION, ALSO MEASURED ON THE CONFORMANCE RUN: a deref whose
# POINTEE TYPE IS A BARE TYPEVAR. `copy_tvs_` is built from the FUNCTION's own
# type params, so an IMPL- or TRAIT-level `T: Copy` is invisible and
# `is_move_type` calls a bare `T` move by default (DIVERGENCES §B1) — sound for
# the partial-move TRACKER it was written for, an over-refusal read as a rule.
# `fn greater_than_one<T: NumExt>(n: &T) -> bool { return *n > one; }`
# (imported/pass/traits/inheritance-num1-b150) is the program. Nothing is lost:
# every instantiation is checked with a concrete type.
# ⚠ THE COST THE PROBE MEASURED WAS THE MISSING RAW EXEMPTION, not the
# place-base confusion the probe's own note guessed at. spec/pass/coerce_3's
# single refusal minimises to a user `struct Cell` + `fn get`, whose mangled
# name collides with the stdlib's `impl<T: Copy> Cell<T>` and drags that
# PRE-MONO generic body into the check: `unsafe { return *self.inner.get(); }`,
# whose operand is `*mut T`. Measured, not argued.
# EXEMPTIONS CHECKED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS by constructed programs, not only by the
# corpus (which refuses only what it contains): a Copy deref, a Copy field
# through `(*r).f`, `&*r`, `**r` over `&&i64`, a raw-pointer move-out, a `Copy`
# static read by value, `&STATIC` and `STATIC.field`, a `Box` field read and
# `&*b`, `match *r { E::A(ref d) => … }`, a `T: Copy` impl-level generic body,
# and a struct destructure `let P { a, b } = *r;` — all still compile.
# The whole 53-target build (liblogos-lang / -mem / -std, memoria, lforge, the
# examples) is the other oracle and it relinked green.
# ⚠ THIS IS NOT A CLASS FIX AND THE ROOT COLUMN SAYS SO. The eleven carry FIVE
# different roots (bck.NEW 7, nllmoves.B 1, bck.B 1, bck.D 1, nllmoves.D 1 — no
# lifereg row at all), because the root column records what the IMPORTING slice
# proposed, and E0507-at-a-deref was never one of the proposals. Class B goes
# 63 -> 61 and bck.NEW 36 -> 29; neither number is evidence about the mechanism.
# bck.NEW is exactly the root the batch report called unexamined, and seven of
# its 36 turn out to be one rule.
# TOTAL 412


